    body: `
<p>At a Dhaka market you count some things and weigh others. English says "four mangoes" and "two kilos of rice". Bengali counts with the classifier -টা on the number, and a measure word like কেজি takes the classifier's place, with no word for "of".</p>
<h3>Asking the price</h3>
<p><b>দাম</b> (<i>dam</i>) is "price" and <b>কত</b> (<i>kôto</i>) is "how much". There is no verb for "is". The thing you ask about takes the -র form.</p>
{{ex:দাম কত?|dam kôto?|What's the price?}}
{{ex:এটার দাম কত?|eṭar dam kôto?|How much is this?}}
{{ex:এক কেজি আলুর দাম কত?|æk keji alur dam kôto?|How much is a kilo of potatoes?}}
<p>To ask about one item, point and use এটার, "this one's". For food sold by weight, the price is per kilo, so ask about এক কেজি.</p>
<p>The answer is a number and টাকা, the Bangladeshi taka. Prices on signs may be in Bengali digits or in Western digits.</p>
{{ex:পঞ্চাশ টাকা।|pônchash ṭaka.|Fifty taka.}}
<h3>Counting and weighing</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Bengali</th></tr><tr><td>four mangoes</td><td class="k">চারটা আম</td></tr><tr><td>one kilo of potatoes</td><td class="k">এক কেজি আলু</td></tr><tr><td>two kilos of rice</td><td class="k">দুই কেজি চাল</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Counted things: number plus -টা. Weighed things: number, কেজি, then the food. Never both. Things you buy one by one are counted; rice and potatoes are weighed. <b>চাল</b> (<i>chal</i>) is uncooked rice, what you buy. ভাত is cooked rice, what you eat.</p>
{{ex:আমাকে চারটা আম দিন।|amake charṭa am din.|Four mangoes, please.}}
{{ex:আমাকে দুই কেজি চাল দিন।|amake dui keji chal din.|Two kilos of rice, please.}}
<h3>Half</h3>
<p><b>অর্ধেক</b> (<i>ôrdhek</i>) is "half" of something. For half a kilo you will usually hear <b>আধা কেজি</b> (<i>adha keji</i>).</p>
{{ex:আমাকে অর্ধেক দিন।|amake ôrdhek din.|Give me half, please.}}
<h3>Too expensive</h3>
<p><b>বেশি</b> (<i>beshi</i>) is "too much" or "more". In a market some bargaining is normal, and this is how it starts. Keep the polite form with the seller: দিন, not the familiar দাও. In a shop with fixed prices, just pay. A price ending in টাকা needs no verb, so পঞ্চাশ টাকা on its own is a full answer.</p>
{{ex:দাম খুব বেশি।|dam khub beshi.|The price is too high.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving the classifier off a counted number. <b>চার আম দিন।</b> → <b>চারটা আম দিন।</b> Counted things take -টা. Only a measure such as কেজি replaces it.</div>
`,
